# G033 — FTP Program

*v2. See `ftp-program.md` for prose: first client-server protocol,
command language as external InnateScript-shaped channel, virtual
filesystem as remote resource behind the protocol, every @reference
shaped like a request-response.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Agent Vault Transfer

[vault-transfer @server-host
    IN ORDER TO
        drive a remote FTP server from an agent session
        listing, fetching, and storing vault notes through a line protocol
        so the transfer commits a set of files atomically or resists

    @client <- [ftp[connect {host: @server-host}]]
        || -> #cannot-connect @resistance

    @listing <- [ftp[send {client: @client, command: "LIST"}]]
        where response begins with "226" -> parse file list
        otherwise                        -> #list-failed

    for @name in @listing
        @resp <- [ftp[send {client: @client,
                             command: "RETR " + @name}]]
            where response begins with "226"
                -> [vault[save {path: "incoming/" + @name,
                                 content: @resp:body}]]
            otherwise
                -> #transfer-error @resp

    for @upload in [vault[outgoing-notes]]
        [ftp[send {client: @client,
                    command: "STOR " + @upload:name + " " +
                              @upload:content}]]

    [ftp[send {client: @client, command: "QUIT"}]]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[ftp
    connect          {host}                    ;; -> Client
    send             {client, command}          ;; -> response string
    disconnect       {client}
]

[ftp-server
    new              {initial-files}
    handle           {server, command}          ;; -> response string
    files            {server}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: command dispatch `where` verb determines response

[ftp-server[handle {server: @s, command: @cmd}]
    IN ORDER TO
        parse @cmd into verb + args and produce the protocol response
        per the FTP reply-code convention (2xx ok, 5xx client error)

    @verb <- first word of @cmd, uppercased
    @verb
        where "LIST"
            -> "226 File list:\n" + sorted filenames joined by newline
        where "RETR"
            -> @name <- second word
               @s:files at @name
                   where present   -> "226 Transfer complete:\n" + content
                   otherwise       -> "550 " + @name + ": No such file"
        where "STOR"
            -> @name, @content <- words 2 and 3
               store @content at @name
               -> "226 " + @name + " stored (" + size + " bytes)"
        where "DELE"
            -> remove @name if present
               -> "250 deleted" or "550 No such file"
        where "QUIT"
            -> "221 Goodbye"
        otherwise
            -> "502 Command not implemented: " + @verb
]

Reply codes are themselves a small sum type: 2xx success, 5xx client
error. Each response carries both a status and optional payload — the
same shape every Innate resolver verb produces (result or resistance).

## Distinctive Innate idiom: protocol as resolver boundary

The FTP protocol IS the resolver boundary: outside the protocol the
client cannot observe server state; inside the protocol every access
is a named verb with a typed response. InnateScript's `@reference`
does exactly this — remote state is reachable only via the command
language the resolver exposes.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @srv <- [ftp-server[new {initial-files: {"readme": "hello"}}]]

    -> [ftp-server[handle {server: @srv, command: "LIST"}]]
    ;; -> "226 File list:\nreadme"

    -> [ftp-server[handle {server: @srv, command: "RETR readme"}]]
    ;; -> "226 Transfer complete:\nhello"

    -> [ftp-server[handle {server: @srv,
                             command: "STOR notes hello-world"}]]
    ;; -> "226 notes stored (11 bytes)"

    -> [ftp-server[handle {server: @srv, command: "QUIT"}]]
    ;; -> "221 Goodbye"
]
